     "id": "9890",
     "word": "márdati",
     "gloss": "pounds, crushes, treads on, squeezes, rubs",
     "native": null,
     "phonemic": null,
     "notes": "",
     "etymology": "<number>9890</number> <b>márdati</b> 'pounds, crushes, treads on, squeezes, rubs' MBh. 2. <b>*mr̥ndati.</b> [<i>mr̥dnā́ti</i> (= <i><a data-entry=\"9890\">márdati</a></i>) MBh., and <i>marmartu</i> RV. (if &lt; <i>°rttu</i>). — In some NIA. lggs. <i><a data-entry=\"9890\">márdati</a></i> and <i>*mr̥ndati</i> have coalesced so that it is not possible in all cases to distinguish from which the modern word stems; e.g. Or. <i>māṛibā</i> may be either &lt; <i>mard</i>- or &lt; <i>mr̥nd</i>- (with <i>mā̃</i>- written as <i><a data-entry=\"9981\">mā</a></i>-); Si. <i>man̆ḍ</i>-, <i>maḍ</i>- may be either &lt; <i>mr̥nd</i>- or &lt; <i>mard</i>- with nasality from initial <i>m</i>-. Inter-dialectal spread of forms accounts for further confusions: e.g. Paš. <i>muṇḍ</i>- ~ <i>maṇḍ</i>- are both prob. &lt; <i>*mr̥ndati</i>. Semantically there is also overlap with <smallcaps><a data-entry=\"9839\">mánthati.</a> — √<a data-entry=\"r462\">mr̥d</a></smallcaps>]",
     "order": 150794,
     "language_id": "Indo-Aryan",
